Description: Open Windows programs in their app, even where executables may not run
 KIO refused to open a .exe or .msi from a browser's downloads ("For
 security reasons, launching executables is not allowed in this
 context") before reaching its own rule that opens Windows programs in
 their associated app. A Windows program cannot run on Linux by itself;
 on Wild OS it opens in the App Bridge, which says what will happen and
 asks before anything runs. The exception is moved ahead of the refusal;
 native programs are refused exactly as before.
Author: Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>
Forwarded: not-needed

--- a/src/gui/openurljob.cpp
+++ b/src/gui/openurljob.cpp
@@ -385,6 +385,15 @@
     isNativeBinary = !mimeType.inherits(QStringLiteral("application/x-ms-dos-executable"));
 #endif
 
+    // Wild OS: a Windows program cannot run on Linux by itself; it is a
+    // document its associated app opens (Wild OS's App Bridge, which says
+    // what will happen and asks first). So it opens there even where
+    // launching executables is not allowed, as from a browser's downloads.
+    if (!isNativeBinary) {
+        openInPreferredApp();
+        return;
+    }
+
